00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.910 align:start position:0%
 
meet the fellows event  the first talk of the last session is uh  by eric mazumba from catholic  cool  yeah thank you  um and so today i'm gonna be talking  about  kind of a line of work on learning in  the presence of strategic agents  and i'm going to start really really  high level and then hopefully i'll get  down into some of the details um but i  just want to start with this very  obvious observation that we're  increasingly interacting with learning  algorithms either unknowingly or  knowingly every single day so when we're  you know ordering something on ubereats  or reading the cartoon on the new yorker  we're implicitly interacting with some  form of a learning algorithm i think  this just goes to show how these  algorithms have just been completely  deployed across the real world  um but as they're actually implemented  in real world settings you know they can  really struggle and in particular they  can struggle in the face of strategic  behaviors so this is one of my favorite  examples it's about a phenomenon known  as surge clubs which happens on ride  sharing markets and so what happens i'll  explain it a little bit is a group of  drivers will all um kind of band  together on uber and they'll go to a  certain area of the city like beverly  hills and they'll turn off their apps at  the same time  then the uber surge pricing algorithm  notices that there's been a sudden dip  in supply  and it'll start surging the price in  that location to attract more drivers  there and then drivers turn on their  apps and then collect the higher search  price  so  you know as a system designers is not  all what i intended because these  drivers are effectively gaming my  algorithm but i think this just goes to  show um the types of behaviors that that  you know algorithms are faced with when  they're deployed into the real world  so  you know this is the type of problem  that i'm interested in kind of  developing algorithms that are robust to  these type of strategic behaviors and  that can reason the face of these  strategic behaviors  so in today's talk i'm going to talk  about kind of three different lines of  work very at a very very high level and  i've kind of aligned them with the three  main topics that will go on in the  learning and games semester at simon's  the first is going to be about  gradient-based algorithms and continuous  games i'll touch very briefly on some of  the implications for multi-agent  reinforced learning and then i'll end on  some  kind of higher level work more kind of  practical work on learning in the  presence of strategic agents  so i'm going to start with  gradient-based algorithms and continuous  games  and the high level question i want you  to keep in mind here is what can we say  about the convergence of gradient-based  learning algorithms in non-convex games  where we lose any types of structure  that have been kind of  classically  been relied on in the learning and games  community and this is kind of a long  line of work  that i've been doing  from my phd all the way to now  with collaborators here at berkeley  and uw  so  um i'm just gonna you know assume that  by now you've seen the definition of  nash equilibria today and so i'll just  kind of give you an idea of the types of  results we get so what we show using  basically dynamical systems and control  theory  is that in non-convex general sum games  gradient play will almost surely avoid a  subset of the nash equilibrium of the  game  and basically what we show is that  nash equilibria can be locally just  straight up unstable in general some  games meaning that even if you  initialize in an arbitrarily small  neighborhood of a nash equilibrium  you'll still diverge and that's because  we can show  that nash equilibria can have basically  the set of initializations from which  you'll converge to nash equilibrium will  just be measure zero in the joint  strategy space  in the context of zero-sum games we show  something a little bit more um kind of  pleasant which is that nash equilibria  are generically stable in zero sum games  and so any non-convergence issues that  you get there are basically just the  results of poor discretizations and  that's kind of nice because it means  that nash equilibria you can always get  you know local last iterate convergence  by doing some kind of  smart discretization tricks like let's  say the proximal point algorithm or  optimism  okay so the second result is that um  in non-convex general some games agents  who basically try and use gradient play  can converge to spurious stationary  points  and so this means that they could  converge to you know local equal  equilibria where in the worst case every  agent has a direction in which they  unilaterally like to deviate to lower  their cost but they can't because of the  uh the algorithms that they're using so  we think of these as being kind of  pretty undesirable equilibria because  they're not necessarily even locally you  know rational  and as from the point this was also  shown at the same time  in the context of zero sum games by by  several people  so  you know i present to this and i think  it's very important to you know point  out that these issues are not just of  purely theoretical interest  and to make it practical i want to you  know take a step into  kind of multi-agent reinforcement  learning world  so you know i don't think anyone here  needs to  a reminder of all the successes of deep  reinforcement learning but you know in  all of these games where deep mind is  playing let's say starcraft and open ai  is playing dota 2. if we look under the  hood the algorithms that they're using  are multi-agent reinforcement algorithms  and in particular they're policy grading  algorithms  so you know we decided to ask a very  simple question which is do policy  grading works in just even simple uh  continuous action space in state space  games so  you know we also looked at you know in  general some linear quadratic games  which if you saw from kaiking's talk um  just before  um i'll  kind of go over what they are so  an lq game is just the generalization of  the lqr problem from kind of classic  control theory and it's you know a very  simple game there's a shared discrete  time dynamical system  um all players have an input to this  dynamical system  and then every player has a loss  function that they want to minimize  which is quadratic in the state of the  system and their own inputs  and then  they're going to optimize over the space  of linearly parametrized policies  so it's a very simple game the space of  kind of  kind of continuous actions and  continuous states  and our goal is just to understand what  happens if you use policy gaining  algorithms in these games  so you know we can show these games are  non-convex we can show that  all local equilibria um for policy  grading algorithms have to be  uh nash equilibria but if we use  policy-grade algorithms in these games  we see that we they converge to kind of  very  non-trivial behaviors in particular  um they converge to cycles and i think  something that i think really sets this  apart from a lot of the work that we  have seen during the workshop and the  bootcamp is that averaging doesn't work  here you can see that on average which  is this dark line  um they don't converge to the nash  equilibrium they convert to something  else  and that's  just because of the fact that you know  these nash equilibria are unstable and  in you know dimension higher than two  you don't have any guarantees that  inside of a limit cycle you have an  equilibrium  so you know we showed that this actually  happened in kind of a large set of  of linear quadratic games in particular  like just by randomly sampling we saw  that this happened  in up to one in four um kind of  general sum multi-agent games  so  you know policy gradients might not be  the way forward  so  you know the takeaway from these first  two parts is that current machine  learning algorithms have no guarantees  of finding game relevant solutions in  continuous games without assuming more  structure  um and if non-confidence and i think an  interesting question is that if  non-convergence isn't a discretization  issue  will optimism and proximal methods still  work  and then the second question is you know  for what classes of games can we  actually give convergence guarantees  um so i you know we saw from kaishin  that they can actually give global  convergence of policy-grade algorithms  in zero-sum games so that was a really  nice result  and then in nurbs this year we were able  to show that in classes of non-convex  non-concave zero-sum games we can  actually give last iterate convergence  to local min max equilibria so  equilibrium that satisfy certain  curvature properties  as long as the two players have a  certain finite time scale separation and  that's kind of using  some interesting kind of lyapunov theory  great and so in the remaining time i  just want to  move on to kind of some much more recent  work  on learning in the presence of strategic  agents and in particular we'd be looking  at the problem of strategic  classification so  um the question kind of overarching  question here  is how should we learn in strategic  classification if strategic agents  gradually adapt to our deployed models  so  the general setup in strategic  classification is that of you know you  have a decision maker and strategic  agents a decision maker wants to  minimize its prediction loss strategic  agents want a favorable predictions out  of this decision maker  and you know they'll deploy the decision  maker deploys a model and then strategic  agents you know will respond  so  in particular strategic agents and  decision makers will adapt to each  other's actions  so you know we ask the question what  happens if this is repeated over and  over and over over time and in  particular if we you know unroll time  and we allow agents and the decision  maker to update you know at perhaps  different time scales  so  what will happen if they're both  adapting to each other over time over  different time scales  and i want to point out that the vast  majority of password and strategic  classification always models agents as  playing an instantaneous best response  so not gradually adapting over time but  always optimally adapting so this is  kind of the main difference in our model  um and so  you know we we kind of make this  observation that  increasingly now because decision makers  are you know tech companies who are  deploying algorithms they can often  choose how fast they learn relative to  the population of users  so in particular a decision maker can be  much much slower relative to the agents  or much much faster relative to the  agents  and you know at a high level what we  show is that if agents  are basically no regret learners  then these two scenarios where the  decision maker is faster where the nurse  is making it slow will lead to  drastically different game theoretic  equilibrium in particular they'll lead  to different stackable equilibria of the  underlying game one  where the decision maker leads and the  other one where the decision maker  follows  and so really the takeaway here is that  the relative learning rate can be used  to select for specific stockoberg  equilibria and strategic classification  so  at a high level the takeaway is just  that when you're learning in the  presence of adaptive no regret strategic  agents the learner the learning rate  doesn't only affect the speed of  convergence to a solution  it actually also affects the type of  solution that you converge to i think  that's a really important um  point to hammer home and so  you know moving forward i think that  there's this really interesting question  which is what other benign design  choices are we making when we are  playing algorithms that actually affect  performance and competitive settings  and so you know i think i want to wrap  up there and just i'm very excited about  you know this entire  semester here at simon's and so if you  have any questions i'd be happy to  answer them and thanks for listening  [Applause]  [Music]  i just uh  i'm just wondering like for the the  second part of the talk when you talk  about uh learning at the faster rate or  a slower rate do we know if the the  convergence point is a nash equilibrium  the next speaker is anish agarwal

